WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:25.470
So I'm going to start talking about the second
part of the data wrangling, normalization,

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:32.110
and preprocessing of text, which is followup
of the talk given by Professor Picone two

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:42.100
weeks ago in which he expressed the deep learning
methods that are used to process the signal.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.530
So clinical encephalography, or EEGs, as I'm
going to call it from now on, is known to

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:52.180
be the most important investigation in the
diagnosis and management of epilepsies.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:57.500
An EEG records the electrical activity along
the scalp and measures spontaneous electric

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.559
activity of the brain.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.930
And for that, it is the main test for brain-related
illnesses.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:12.210
In my talk, I'm going to show how, along with
the EEG signals, we are processing the EEG

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.380
reports which are documenting the interpretation
of these signals.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:26.229
So I'm going to show the kinds of methods
that are used in order to process the clinical

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:29.870
text in the EEG reports.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:35.670
So data wrangling is defined as the process
of mapping data from an unstructured format--

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.609
in this case, the narratives of the EEG reports--
to another format that enables automatic processing,

00:01:41.609 --> 00:01:47.619
and in this talk today, why do we need to
see how that format is basically a very complex

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:54.570
annotation schema that enables us to do two
kinds of processing-- one, to organize the

00:01:54.570 --> 00:02:01.090
information of complex probabilistic graph
that would enable probabilistic inference,

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:08.200
and the other one on which I'm going to focus
more is in representing these EEG reports

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:15.420
and the signals into multimodal index that
would enable us to perform identification

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.980
of patient cohorts.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:24.370
So the ability to process the narratives in
electronic medical records, which are now

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:30.480
collected in every hospital in the country,
would also give rise to the identification

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.849
of a staggering wealth of biomedical knowledge.

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:40.790
This knowledge will be inferred not only from
the abstracted text, but also from many measurements

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:47.430
such as vital signs or multichannel signal
data-- as in our case, it's provided by the

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.099
EEG signals.

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:54.849
So we believe that this information could
be transformative if properly harnessed.

00:02:54.849 --> 00:02:59.489
So as I'm going to be focusing mostly on processing
the clinical test, we are going to see that

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:07.629
current state-of-the-art natural language
processing need to be enhanced with deep learning

00:03:07.629 --> 00:03:11.799
methods that face the challenges which are
posed by the complexity and the volume of

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:16.300
big data repositories of medical records.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.030
So I'm going to show how combining the advantages
of two kinds of learning-- namely, active

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:28.250
and deep learning-we will be able to process
a large collection of EEG reports and annotate

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:35.090
with the clinical concepts and their attributes,
which will enable us to create an EEG-specific

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:42.370
qualified medical knowledge graph, and also
to be able to use that for medical inference,

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:48.269
like estimations of likelihoods of the clinical
correlations or interpretation of EEG test,

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:52.659
and also in the discovery of patient cohorts.

00:03:52.659 --> 00:04:00.230
Now, when we talk about clinical text mining,
this has been done for many decades.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:07.540
And there's ample research that can be distinguished
in mining the biomedical literature-- for

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.640
instance, trying to find medical concepts
in medical journals, medical textbooks, and

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.540
scholarly research reports, or in another
case, mining the clinical notes-for instance,

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:25.930
radiology reports, progress notes, surgical
notes, or hospital discharge summaries.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:32.530
These two kinds of clinical text mining are
quite different because the first one focuses

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:42.070
on biomedical NLP that extracts genes or proteins
or pathways and biomedical events and relations,

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:47.980
which gives an opportunity for translational
work in biomedical natural language processing--

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:53.630
with other words, getting results from the
biological domain into the clinical domain.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:59.300
When we perform clinical NLP, we're actually
focusing on building profiles of individual

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:06.300
patients by extracting a broad class of medical
concepts and conditions-- for instance, the

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:12.700
diseases, the injuries, or the symptoms and
signs, as well as responses like the diagnosis,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.790
procedures, treatments, and drugs.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.850
And the goal here is to improve patient care.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:24.940
So the talk today will focus on clinical NLP
processing.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:33.910
I want to point out another special case of
performing clinic NLP, which is for aiding

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.000
computerized clinical decision support.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:44.010
And there's an excellent article authored
by Dr. Diana Demner-Fushman, Wendy Chapman,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:49.150
and Clement McDonald that is offering an excellent
survey.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:55.940
The title of this article is "What Can Natural
Language Processing Do for Clinical Decision

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.620
Support?"

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:04.900
Well, let's look at what are the steps of
clinical and classical natural language processing

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.550
of electronic medical records.

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:12.810
As we see, we first need to identify what
are the medical concepts that we want to annotate

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:15.390
or discover in the medical records.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:24.211
In addition, because these narratives in medical
records are often using [INAUDIBLE], we need

00:06:24.211 --> 00:06:26.050
to identify the assertions.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.260
Are these medical concepts factual or are
they negated?

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:34.380
Are they expressing some hypothesis?

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:40.130
After we identify the medical concepts and
infer the assertions, the next step is to

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:46.680
discover the features that would enable us
to recognize them automatically.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:54.590
The features are informing some classifiers
that would be trained and will be able to

00:06:54.590 --> 00:07:00.810
annotate automatically the medical concepts
in the records.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:07.220
The results of these classifiers also need
to be evaluated, and the cycle continues.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:18.120
Well, there's very interesting work and excellent
resources on discovering medical records and

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.980
natural language processing.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:30.090
And there was a challenge in 2010, for instance,
that aimed at recognize automatically in records

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.150
medical problems, medical tests, and treatments.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:41.400
So we wanted to take advantage of this past
work and understand that recognizing such

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.990
concepts involved two decisions.

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:49.720
First of all, you need to identify the boundaries
of these concepts-- with other words, what

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.030
is the first word in the
narrative where the concept is mentioned,

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.830
and what is the last word?

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:03.240
And after you recognize the boundaries, well,
what kind of medical concepts have we recognized--

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.720
with other words, what's the type classification?

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:11.390
Is it a problem, is it a test, or is it a
treatment?

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:17.090
So the problem is and the challenge is that
we are facing when we need to annotate big

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:21.690
data is that this time, we're having to annotate
many more types.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:28.470
There are resources that perform wonderfully,
for instance, [INAUDIBLE] that would identify

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:34.790
in medical records any concept that is annotated
in the Unified Medical Language System.

00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:41.419
However, there is not coverage for any kind
of EEG-related medical concepts there.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:47.570
So we have to go and look at other annotations
that we can provide for the specific type

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.710
of records.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:57.149
As many more types of medical concepts will
need to be discovered in big data that would

00:08:57.149 --> 00:09:01.970
infer that we'll have to have a very large
number of classifiers, and that poses an incredible

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:07.370
challenge because those classifiers will need
to have some optimal feature selection, and

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:13.210
it is very difficult to do that throughout
a large number of classifiers.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:18.670
So the solution comes from deep learning and
active learning.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.930
And by combining deep learning and active
learning, we're actually generating a multitask

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:30.520
active deep learning paradigm that aims to
perform concurrently multiple annotations

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:37.620
of all the EEG activities and their attributes,
or the EEG events, or the medical problems,

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.170
medical tests, and medical treatments that
are encountered in the EEG reports.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.980
Well, what are EEG activities?

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:49.970
These are brain activities such as waves and
patterns.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.779
And what they are the EEG events?

00:09:53.779 --> 00:10:01.990
These are brain responses as direct result
of a specific sensory, cognitive, or [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:03.070
event.

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:10.079
In addition, with the identification of every
medical concept of these types, we want to

00:10:10.079 --> 00:10:13.110
identify their modality and polarity values.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:16.730
What are the possible values of modality?

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:22.699
It's factual, possible, or proposed, which
indicate whether that clinical concept is

00:10:22.699 --> 00:10:28.980
an actual finding or is it possible finding,
or a finding that may be true at some point

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.120
in time in the future.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:37.110
And polarity has a value of either positive
or negative.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:44.009
On the lower-hand side of this slide, you
are seeing that basically, the annotations

00:10:44.009 --> 00:10:47.529
that we are performing are of multiple types.

00:10:47.529 --> 00:10:54.689
And for instance, in the case of an annotation
for an EEG activity such as rhythm, we have

00:10:54.689 --> 00:11:00.620
a lot of attributes, like this is a rhythm
with a frequency band of alpha, and it is

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:08.920
located in both hemispheres, and it is recurring
continuously, and the location is occipital,

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.410
and the modality is factual, and the polarity
is positive.

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:18.550
So with this, I am exemplifying the complexity
of the many types and the many attributes

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:29.800
that we need to annotate in a set of very
large number of EEG reports.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:33.550
So what's in an EEG report?

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:39.509
When an EEG report is created, it follows
the American Clinical Neurophysiology Society

00:11:39.509 --> 00:11:41.949
guidelines for writing such reports.

00:11:41.949 --> 00:11:47.449
It has to start with a section on the clinical
history describing the patient age, gender,

00:11:47.449 --> 00:11:50.770
and the relevant medical conditions and medications.

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:53.389
Then an introduction section is discussing
the

00:11:53.389 --> 00:11:59.199
EEG technique or configuration, followed by
a description of the notable waveforms, activities,

00:11:59.199 --> 00:12:03.820
patterns, or EEG events from the signal.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:08.290
Then a mandatory section is the impression,
which is interpretation, whether the EEG indicates

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:15.209
normal or abnormal brain activity, and a list
of the contributing [INAUDIBLE] phenomena.

00:12:15.209 --> 00:12:19.740
And it ends with a clinical correlation section,
which relates the findings with the overall

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:24.230
clinical patient picture.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:34.430
So when annotating a big data of EEG reports,
we had to create a schema such that we could

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:41.760
describe all the semantics of the EEG activities
and events as they are discussed in the report.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.500
So the first question that we had to answer
was, well, what medical concept do we really

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.459
need to annotate, and what are their types?

00:12:49.459 --> 00:12:57.260
We decided to annotate medical problems, treatments,
and tests, as well as EEG activities and events.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.839
And you see here our first example.

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:05.410
For instance, if in the clinical history we
are reading recently seizure-free but with

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:13.370
episodes of light flashing in her peripheral
vision followed by a blurry vision and headaches,

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:19.360
we want to be able to annotate the fact that
seizure is a medical problem, as well as episodes

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.970
of light flashing in her peripheral vision
is a medical problem.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:29.839
As you see, this problem for these medical
concepts can be mentioned as a single word,

00:13:29.839 --> 00:13:36.920
seizure, or a sequence of words, like in episodes
of light flashing in her peripheral vision.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:41.870
In the following section, Topamax is identified
as a treatment.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:47.350
Now, when we go to the description of the
record, we are seeing that sharply controlled

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:50.589
delta activity is actually an EEG activity.

00:13:50.589 --> 00:13:53.050
So is spike complex.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:58.430
But around these activities, we have a lot
of other attributes that are expressed, like

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:03.680
the fact that the sharply controlled delta
activity is frontally predominant and it is

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.350
irregular.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:13.140
So we need to capture, also, these attributes
of the EEG activities in our annotation schema.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:20.009
As we know [INAUDIBLE] EEG activities are
not mentioned always in continuous expressions,

00:14:20.009 --> 00:14:27.410
we decided to use a schema in which we will
annotate the anchors of EEG activities, and

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:32.050
the attributes will be determined as a followon.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.410
What kind of attributes we decided to capture?

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:38.460
Well, we decided to capture a very large number
of attributes.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:41.910
The most notable one is the morphology.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:47.029
The morphology represents the type or form
of the EEG [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:47.029 --> 00:14:53.470
You see here that on the right-hand side of
the slide, I'm showing the semantic hierarchy

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:59.870
of the values that express the morphology
of the EEG activities.

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:04.500
They are classified as rhythms.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.920
They may be transient.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.920
They may be a single wave, like for instance,
wicket spikes.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.920
They may be complex.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:19.449
And as complex waveforms, they may be spike
and sharp wave complexes or sharp and slow

00:15:19.449 --> 00:15:21.139
wave complexes.

00:15:21.139 --> 00:15:28.339
But they can also express a large variety
of patterns, like suppression or like a periodic

00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:32.809
lateralized epileptic form discharges.

00:15:32.809 --> 00:15:38.209
The morphology is just one of the attributes,
along with several others, that describe the

00:15:38.209 --> 00:15:39.640
general attributes.

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:47.600
We also have temporal attributes and spatial
attributes that we want to annotate.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:53.290
The frequency bands represent the second attribute
that we annotate that distinguish whether

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:58.699
the waveform is an alpha, beta, delta, or
theta or gamma form.

00:15:58.699 --> 00:16:02.999
We also want to annotate whether this is in
the background or not.

00:16:02.999 --> 00:16:09.129
And as general attributes, we also annotate
separately the magnitudes, which describes

00:16:09.129 --> 00:16:12.139
the amplitude of the EEG activity.

00:16:12.139 --> 00:16:21.869
Among the temporal attributes, we are annotating
the recurrence-- showing how often the EEG

00:16:21.869 --> 00:16:22.949
activity occurs.

00:16:22.949 --> 00:16:27.929
And the possible values are it's continuous,
repeated, or if it is none, that means that

00:16:27.929 --> 00:16:31.339
it occurs only once.

00:16:31.339 --> 00:16:37.309
The EEG activities are also associated with
a significant number of spatial attributes,

00:16:37.309 --> 00:16:40.680
and they are grouped in three categories.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:45.279
The first category is the dispersal attributes,
which describe the spread of the activity

00:16:45.279 --> 00:16:49.550
over regions of the brain-- could be localized
or generalized.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:54.850
The second type of spatial attributes are
the hemisphere where it occurs-- could be

00:16:54.850 --> 00:17:02.139
right, left, or both-- and finally, in a [INAUDIBLE]
spatial brain, the brain location, which has

00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:07.640
another number of attributes describing whether
it's frontal, occipital, and so forth.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:14.100
So as you see, we have a total of 16 attributes
that are associated with each EEG activity,

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:20.410
which makes for a very complex semantics of
the annotate schema.

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:23.210
So how would these annotations look like?

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:28.340
In this slide, you see an example of the manual
annotations of the EEG reports that we have

00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:34.170
performed to create training data for our
deep learning architectures.

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:38.200
In this example, you see two types of annotations.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:41.790
The blue annotations represent the boundaries.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:49.450
For instance, multiple sclerosis indicate
that that is a mention of medical concepts.

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:54.760
In red, you see the actual type of the medical
concept.

00:17:54.760 --> 00:18:00.760
So here, we see it's multiple sclerosis, it
is a medical problem, it is having the modality

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:05.820
of factual, and it is having the polarity
of positive.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:12.560
But please note when we go into the introduction
section how the annotations become more complex

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:19.410
because, for instance, in the case of the
EEG activities, like spike and wave discharges,

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:25.710
in red, we see all these values of the various
attributes that are associated with it that

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:32.520
need to be annotated, like the morphology
is transient, it is a complex wave in the

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:39.890
form of spike and slow wave complex with a
frequency band of delta, not in the background,

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:45.840
with a magnitude normal, that recurs as repeated,
the disperse is generalized.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:50.160
We don't have information about the hemisphere,
but we have information about the location

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:52.260
as being frontal.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:56.880
And the modality is factual, and the polarity
is positive.

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:02.990
I want to note that in this annotation, we
are actually making sure that we're attacking

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:12.660
two important text processing problem-- namely,
the ambiguity and the economicity of text.

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:19.950
Ambiguity means that we may have one annotation
that may end up expressing different semantic

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.230
points in the hierarchy.

00:19:22.230 --> 00:19:30.220
And economicity means that in this specific
EEG report, there's no place where it's indicated

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:37.900
that this is a frequency band of delta that
we need to infer because later on, we're basically

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:45.429
seeing that the notation is from 4 to 4.5
hertz.

00:19:45.429 --> 00:19:51.860
So you'll see it's a very
complex annotation schema that tries to capture

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:57.410
the wealth of semantic information that is
described in the EEG report.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:20:02.680
So let's see how we can create some machine
learning architectures that would enable us

00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:09.360
to automatically annotate a very large corpus
of EEG reports following a schema.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:16.700
First of all, we need to preprocess the EEG
reports because there's many very important

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:21.300
resources for medical language processing
out there.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:28.000
So we use the GENIA resources, which comprise
a tagger for tokenization-- tokenization means

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:35.770
identifying every word in the narrative--
lemmatization, which means every word is transformed

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:41.510
into its base lemma form, the part of speech
which recognize and that this word is a noun

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:46.780
and this words is a verb, and so forth, as
well as phrase chunking, which means how the

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.300
words are grammatically sticking together
in a phrase.

00:20:50.300 --> 00:21:00.120
We also use the widely popular resource, which
is the Stanford CoreNLP, for detecting syntactic

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:02.160
dependency parsing.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:11.360
In addition, because every token or word in
the EEG reports were identified, we created

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:17.790
the Brown cluster features from the entire
Temple University Hospital cohorts.

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:23.760
The Brown clustering is an unsupervised learning
method that discover hierarchical classes

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:26.580
of words based on the context in which they
occur.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:33.860
We also made use of the Unified Medical Language
System to create feature vector representations

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:39.180
that capture the medical knowledge that is
available from this system.

00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:42.890
Then we have decided on two groups of features.

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:48.610
The first group will be used in the deep learning
architectures that identified the EEG activity

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:56.310
anchors, as well as the boundaries of the
EEG events, medical problems, medical tests,

00:21:56.310 --> 00:21:58.670
and medical treatments.

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:01.830
We have decided on seven different features.

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:08.500
The first one would be the lemma of the token
and the previous and the next tokens.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:13.840
The second feature is the part of speech of
the current token and its previous and next

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:14.840
token.

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:19.150
The third feature is the phrase chunk in which
that token belongs.

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:23.500
The fourth one is the lemma of the current,
previous, and next token.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:29.120
We made use of a feature that represent the
Brown cluster in which the token is situated,

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:37.630
then the UMLS concept unique identifier for
that token, and the title and the section

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:42.560
of the EEG report where the token is observed.

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:50.490
In order to detect the EEG concept type, the
attributes, the many attributes of the EEG

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:56.480
activities as well as the EEG concept, modality,
and polarity, we decided on another set of

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:02.920
nine features, which are listed here-- the
clinical constant mentioned, the lemmas of

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:07.990
each token, the part of speech, the lemmas
of the three tokens before and after, the

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:13.900
title the section, but this time also used
syntactic dependency paths, the number of

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:18.530
words between the clinical concept mentioned
and the specific token, the number of hops

00:23:18.530 --> 00:23:23.690
in the syntactic dependency path, as well
as the number of clinical concepts between

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:28.690
the clinical concept mentioned and the token
in question.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:36.870
So that corresponded to that block in the
natural language processing cycle in which

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:41.830
we had to decide on the features that best
represent the clinical concepts that we want

00:23:41.830 --> 00:23:48.260
to annotate in a vast resource of records.

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:53.270
So once that we have the features, we have
to go and decide on the deep learning architectures.

00:23:53.270 --> 00:24:00.790
Two weeks ago, Professor Picone discussed
a lot about the art and the magic that is

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:06.400
involved in deciding on the best architectures
that you want to use.

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:14.480
Deep learning is the preferred machine learning
techniques that can be used in the face of

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:22.610
very large collections of text-- in our case,
a very big resource of EEG reports.

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:29.010
So here I'm going to start discussing the
first architectures that we used to identify

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:36.120
the boundaries and to identify the anchors
of the EEG activities.

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:41.370
The boundaries were identified for the EEG
events, the medical problems, the test, and

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:43.770
the treatments.

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:52.850
So in order to decide the architecture that
is best suited to be able to learn how to

00:24:52.850 --> 00:25:02.320
automatically identify these boundaries and
anchors, we have decided to use two architectures--

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:06.940
one for the EEG activity anchors, and the
second one for the boundaries of the other

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:10.600
types of medical concepts that we were aiming
to annotate.

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:18.200
These architectures, as you see on this slide,
are represented by stacked long short-term

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:27.110
memory cells, and they are stacked because
they are having several layers, and we represent

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:32.480
it in this architecture as entries of the
tokens.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:41.060
So you see t1, t2, t3, tn corresponds to the
tokens of every sentence in any section of

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:42.920
the EEG report.

00:25:42.920 --> 00:25:48.860
The output of the architecture [INAUDIBLE]
is labels b1 to bn-- one label bi is associated

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:51.200
with the token ti.

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:55.160
The label values are i or b.

00:25:55.160 --> 00:26:01.940
A label of b indicates that this word is the
beginning of a mention of a clinical concept.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:07.940
A label of i indicates that this word or token
is inside the mention of the clinical concept.

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:14.160
And the label of o indicates that this word
is outside of any kind of clinical concept

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:17.840
that we want to annotate.

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:27.240
So let's go a little bit in more detail on
how this deep architecture, the neural architecture,

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:28.890
actually work.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:34.700
So by considering each of the tokens in each
sentence, we want to predict the labels that

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:36.780
they should be associated with.

00:26:36.780 --> 00:26:43.920
Therefore, we have a better representation
of the token from the sentence.

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:47.730
You might be asking yourself, what is in this
vector representation?

00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:55.710
Every token is associated with all the features
that we have decided we want to use-- so the

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.790
[INAUDIBLE] values for that specific token.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:07.150
And what is the output [INAUDIBLE] is values
for the labels in, out, or beginning of a

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:09.870
medical concept.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:16.640
I want to know the fact that each of the long
short-term memory cells in this architecture

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:22.110
is also using a memory state that is shared
throughout the network.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:28.190
For instance, on the first layer, the LSTM
cell receives an input from the vector of

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:29.310
the token.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.210
So let's take token number 2.

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:38.610
That vector will be input to that LSTM cell,
which also gets as input the memory from the

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:44.710
previous LSTM cell and produces an output
which becomes an input to the next layer,

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:45.710
and so forth.

00:27:45.710 --> 00:27:51.360
So we have such three layers that make sure
that they are propagating all the features

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:59.870
from the current token, as well as the previous
tokens, to the soft-max layers that decides

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:03.630
the labels that should be associated with
every

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:05.790
word.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:08.190
So we use these three labels.

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:14.890
And on the soft-max layer, we are actually
producing a probability distribution of the

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:19.270
possible labels of values i, o, and b.

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:26.010
And this is accomplished using a vector of
all the probabilities of a label to be an

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:30.220
i or a label to be on o or a label to be a
b.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:35.970
Therefore, the soft-max layer is predicting
what is the highest probability of a label

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:39.260
that should be associated with that token.

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:48.470
So this architecture enables us to identify
and to annotate the EEG activity anchors and

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:54.470
the boundaries of all the other medical concepts.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:29:01.220
But we still need to understand what are the
attributes associated with this EEG activities

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:06.070
and what are the types of the other medical
concepts, as well as what are their polarities

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:08.840
and modalities.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:14.990
This is accomplished by developing two other
deep learning architectures.

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:21.840
So you see in this slide that we have one
architecture that would identify all the attributes

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:28.510
of these EEG activities, or the 16 ones that
we have discussed before, as well as the modality

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:29.980
and the polarity.

00:29:29.980 --> 00:29:36.270
The second architecture is identifying a type
of medical concepts divided into medical problems

00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:42.810
or EEG events or treatments or test, as well
as the modality and the polarity.

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:49.110
You can note that these architectures are
very different because of the fact that this

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:56.240
time, while using a deep rectifier linear
network, which is using itself five fully

00:29:56.240 --> 00:30:06.090
connected layers of rectifier linear units,
so why we decided on using this architecture?

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:08.090
Why does it work very well?

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:11.630
And how does it really work?

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:18.570
The decision of using these fully connected
layers of rectifier linear units was based

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:22.860
on the incredible properties that these units
provide.

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:25.130
So I'll call them ReLU from now on.

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:32.200
So ReLUs are known that they allow for a very
easy deep net configuration, but they also

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:38.560
have a sparse representation that allows them
to perform de facto internal [INAUDIBLE] selection.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:45.860
With other words, when you learn this multitask
embedding in this architecture, you are actually

00:30:45.860 --> 00:30:53.511
learning a selection of the optimal pictures
in identifying each of the attributes of the

00:30:53.511 --> 00:30:59.440
EEG activities or in identifying each of the
types of medical concepts that you want to

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:01.730
annotate.

00:31:01.730 --> 00:31:10.710
So these 16 attributes are identified by feeding
with shared embedding into a separate software

00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:18.120
layer for each of the attributes, and the
same thing happens for the two software layers

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:23.680
of identifying the medical concept types and
their modality and polarity.

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:29.220
So instead of training tens of classifiers,
we're actually having only two deep learning

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:39.691
architectures that perform very accurate annotations
on a very large set of EEG reports, and they

00:31:39.691 --> 00:31:45.820
do that with learning the optimal features
that need to be selected.

00:31:45.820 --> 00:31:54.320
Well, as we develop these two types of deep
learning architectures, we want to incorporate

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:57.760
them in the active learning loop.

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:02.690
The active learning loop starts with this
vast collection of EEG reports on which manual

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.970
annotations were performed.

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:07.590
And I've shown you examples of how they look
like.

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:14.680
But those annotations become initial training
data for the two types of deep learning architectures

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:16.080
that we just presented.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:24.580
These architectures then are used to annotate
the thousands and thousands of EEG reports

00:32:24.580 --> 00:32:30.970
automatically, and then we need to have some
sampling methods to decide which of them we

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:37.680
should present to a neurologist expert for
validation and editing.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:46.710
And this enhances the initial training sessions,
and they annotate the entire corpus again.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:52.690
I want to note here on the right-hand side
that the active learning loop is the one in

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:58.910
which you have an expert going and validating
the results of the deep learning annotations

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:01.330
and provides new retraining data.

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:07.240
And in order of making the best use of the
expert, you need to develop some sampling

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:09.060
methods.

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:16.810
And the sampling methods that we have developed
are taking into account that we are making

00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:25.070
many types of annotations, as we needed to
combine a rank combination protocol that would

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:29.340
combine the single task active learning selections
into one.

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:37.300
For this, we developed a usefulness core for
each unvalidated annotation from the EEG reports

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:45.620
and calculated it with the kind of rank it
would allow to have the highest usefulness

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:48.000
to the neurologist experts.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:56.190
We also experimented with some Shannon entropy
in developing these sampling methods.

00:33:56.190 --> 00:34:02.370
So the sampling methods enabled the usage
of the active learning, which will be more

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:06.810
accepting and editing the annotations, and
then retraining the deep learning methods

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:09.860
and evaluate the new system.

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:11.020
How does it work?

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:16.010
Well, on the left-hand side here, you see
the learning curves of all the annotations.

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:23.710
So if you take, for instance, the first 100
EEG reports that were annotated and evaluated,

00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:30.230
you see that, for instance, that the boundary
detection very quickly improves from-- we

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:38.559
start from an F-score of 85%, and we very
quickly go to about 90% only from a small

00:34:38.559 --> 00:34:42.039
set of 100 reports.

00:34:42.039 --> 00:34:47.759
The EEG anchors being more complicated, it
is interesting to see that we start, again,

00:34:47.759 --> 00:34:50.530
from an F-score of around 86%.

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:58.630
The quality decreases initially, but it increases
after a very small set of annotations.

00:34:58.630 --> 00:35:04.849
The attributes which were not expressed in
the anchors and morphology are showing the

00:35:04.849 --> 00:35:05.930
greatest increase.

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:07.700
They the most complex ones.

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:17.690
And we start from a low score of around 67%
F1 score, and we get to almost 75% F-score

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:28.849
in very few number of annotations, which is
also expressed by the activity attributes

00:35:28.849 --> 00:35:30.059
learning curve.

00:35:30.059 --> 00:35:35.609
Now, once the EEG report annotations are being
produced, what are we going to be able to

00:35:35.609 --> 00:35:36.950
do with them?

00:35:36.950 --> 00:35:39.299
We are looking at two possible applications.

00:35:39.299 --> 00:35:47.890
The first one is to create an EEG-specific
qualified medical knowledge graph which can

00:35:47.890 --> 00:35:53.369
be used for medical question-answering as
a clinical decision support, as well as for

00:35:53.369 --> 00:35:58.850
various medical probabilistic inference problems,
like learning the posterior distribution of

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:00.920
clinical correlation given an EEG description.

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:09.190
Alternatively, the EEG report annotations
enable us to create a semantically rich index

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:16.700
of clinical EEGs, and also incorporate the
EEG signal data in order to retrieve patient

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:18.630
cohorts.

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:22.749
So I'm going to focus on the second application
now.

00:36:22.749 --> 00:36:30.619
The EEG report annotations would enable us
to create this semantically rich index because

00:36:30.619 --> 00:36:36.670
for every medical concept that we automatically
identify, we can generate a separate unique

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.960
identifier.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:42.589
Along with the identifier, we'll generate
the concept type.

00:36:42.589 --> 00:36:49.420
For instance, this is an alpha activity, or
it is a sharp and slow wave.

00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:55.339
And if it is a concept that uses multiple
terms, then each term would receive its own

00:36:55.339 --> 00:36:56.339
term ID.

00:36:56.339 --> 00:37:03.170
For the EEG activities, we'll also organize
all the attributes and the terms that I used

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:05.410
to express those attributes.

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:09.529
That would generate what we call a medical
concept dictionary.

00:37:09.529 --> 00:37:17.160
These medical concept dictionaries can also
be associated with their IDs into a term dictionary

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:23.380
because in information retrieval, we generate
term-based dictionaries.

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:30.400
So every term ID that was found associated
with every medical concept ID would be expressed

00:37:30.400 --> 00:37:36.450
in the term dictionary, and that's where we
associate the polarity that we have discovered,

00:37:36.450 --> 00:37:39.750
along with the concept in which the term was
used.

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:46.779
Any time we have a positive polarity, we'll
indicate what EEG report that term was found,

00:37:46.779 --> 00:37:53.490
in which section, in which position in the
section, what was the medical concept ID in

00:37:53.490 --> 00:38:02.509
which it appeared, and what was the actual
concept and their modality, if it was a negated

00:38:02.509 --> 00:38:10.049
concept that it represented with similar information,
but in a different list.

00:38:10.049 --> 00:38:16.990
In information retrieval, we use dictionaries,
and also posting files.

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:22.319
So now I'm going to put my information retrieval
researcher hat on, and we'll discuss, well,

00:38:22.319 --> 00:38:30.460
how can we search for relevant patients, and
how can we really be able to use this index

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:32.670
in the best way?

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:37.410
So we first need to create inverted tiered
lists.

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:39.440
So what are inverted tier lists?

00:38:39.440 --> 00:38:45.300
They make sure that every time when you find,
for instance, a concepts like a seizure, it

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:50.589
would be able to know all of the EEG reports
in which they are encountered.

00:38:50.589 --> 00:38:57.849
So we'll have tiered inverted lists for every
positive polarity indication of seizure, as

00:38:57.849 --> 00:39:05.460
well as another set of inverted lists for
every negative indication of a seizure in

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:10.900
which you see that we have the term dictionaries,
like seizure, but we'll also know that that

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:19.249
specific term belong to a medical concept
ID-- in this case, a medical problem that

00:39:19.249 --> 00:39:24.869
is encountered in some EEG report.

00:39:24.869 --> 00:39:31.000
But as you remember from two weeks ago, Dr.
[INAUDIBLE] showed that these EEG reports

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:36.460
are associated with every EEG signal and their
processing.

00:39:36.460 --> 00:39:44.470
So we'd like to be able to also use this other
modality that shows the actual tests, and

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:52.829
we created some EEG signal fingerprints that
are also linked into the tiered inverted lists

00:39:52.829 --> 00:40:02.359
that would enable us to search for the patients
that we are interested as based on the criterias

00:40:02.359 --> 00:40:05.390
of the cohorts.

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:11.930
The EEG fingerprints are also organized in
a similarity hierarchy.

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:13.619
So how does this work?

00:40:13.619 --> 00:40:21.839
Well, the EEG fingerprints are generated by
taking into account that the EEG signal includes

00:40:21.839 --> 00:40:27.900
this floating-point matrix based on the number
of electrode channels that were used, as well

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:31.070
as the number of samples in the recording.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:37.940
So when we did one pass over all the EEG signals,
we found that this requires an enormous amount

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:39.650
of information.

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:46.289
Just for one signal, we would need to have
more than 400 gigabytes of information.

00:40:46.289 --> 00:40:51.930
Therefore, for the multimodal index, we have
to consider a more compact representation

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.710
of these EEG fingerprints.

00:40:54.710 --> 00:40:59.849
This compact representation was created by
another deep learning architecture.

00:40:59.849 --> 00:41:06.440
So now you see a third exemplification of
big learning in which all the EEG signals

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:11.180
are processed in a very short time.

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:18.890
Instead of processing them in weeks and weeks,
we are processing them in matters of hours

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:25.810
because we reduced the EEG signal from 20
megabytes to just a few hundred bytes.

00:41:25.810 --> 00:41:31.630
That was possible by using the recurring neural
network that you see on this slide in which

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:38.180
we consider that every EEG signal is actually
a sequence of samples such that for every

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:40.869
sample, we'll learn how to predict the next
sample.

00:41:40.869 --> 00:41:46.940
And this was made possible by using, again,
the long short-term memory cells that would

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:51.180
learn longrange interactions in the EEG signal.

00:41:51.180 --> 00:41:58.100
So the long short-term memory cells maintain
an update in internal memory such that they

00:41:58.100 --> 00:42:07.249
can output the EEG fingerprint after they
look at all the samples.

00:42:07.249 --> 00:42:16.930
So once that we have created this complex
multimodal index, we can answer natural language

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:22.210
queries that express the criteria for a patient
cohort.

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:29.299
For instance, if a neurologist would like
to find a query of patients that take Topiramate,

00:42:29.299 --> 00:42:37.220
Topamax, with a diagnosis of headaches and
an EEG demonstrating sharp wave spikes or

00:42:37.220 --> 00:42:44.099
spike [INAUDIBLE] wave activity, the deep
learning methods that process the clinical

00:42:44.099 --> 00:42:49.420
texts would be able to annotate this query
and recognize that Topiramate, Topamax are

00:42:49.420 --> 00:42:58.369
medicines, and that headache is a problem,
and the EEG is a test, and sharp wave spikes

00:42:58.369 --> 00:43:03.320
and spike [INAUDIBLE] are EEG activities.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:09.099
Once that the analysis of the query is being
performed from the EEG cohort description,

00:43:09.099 --> 00:43:14.299
we'll be able to use two relevance models
that take into account the term or the concept

00:43:14.299 --> 00:43:21.069
dictionary that we have discussed, as well
as the inverted list to enable the recognition

00:43:21.069 --> 00:43:22.650
of patient cohorts.

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:28.829
So we experimented with two cases-- one case
in which we used only the annotations on the

00:43:28.829 --> 00:43:34.980
clinical texts, and another case in which
we augmented those annotations with the index

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:40.769
that considered the signal, as well-- therefore,
the multimodal index.

00:43:40.769 --> 00:43:46.930
And we were pleasantly surprised that this
relevance model showed that the multimodal

00:43:46.930 --> 00:43:50.119
index produced the better cohorts.

00:43:50.119 --> 00:43:56.809
So in order for [INAUDIBLE] these cohorts,
we had to develop relevance models.

00:43:56.809 --> 00:44:03.960
In informational retrieval, there's this BM55
ranking function that enables you to find

00:44:03.960 --> 00:44:11.980
the most relevant documents or EEG reports
that would match a certain query.

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:19.539
If we are considering also the signal fingerprint,
then we had to develop another relevance model

00:44:19.539 --> 00:44:25.530
in which we could look up the most similar
EEG fingerprints for the top rank reports

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:30.440
provided by the BM25 ranking function.

00:44:30.440 --> 00:44:37.700
So we asked, then, several neurologists to
provide with a patient cohort descriptions,

00:44:37.700 --> 00:44:43.140
which we call queries, and
here you see five examples of descriptions

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:48.490
that they all agreed that would be of high
clinical interest for them.

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:54.450
And then we passed those queries through our
patient cohort retrieval system and asked

00:44:54.450 --> 00:44:57.940
them to evaluate the results.

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:05.579
So for every patient cohort criteria, we identified
the 10 most relevant EEG reports, and then

00:45:05.579 --> 00:45:10.499
we did a random sample of 10 other reports
that were retrieved between the ranks of 11

00:45:10.499 --> 00:45:11.660
and 100.

00:45:11.660 --> 00:45:17.880
Each neurologist was asked to judge whether
each of the EEG report was considered relevant.

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:23.099
They would give a rank of 1 if the patient
described in the report definitely belong

00:45:23.099 --> 00:45:29.859
to the cohort they were looking for, and a
0 if the patient does not belong to that cohort.

00:45:29.859 --> 00:45:35.940
And then we used three measures that are widely
accepted in the information retrieval community

00:45:35.940 --> 00:45:38.579
to evaluate the quality of the results.

00:45:38.579 --> 00:45:44.810
These measures are the mean average precision,
the normalized discounted cumulative gain,

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:46.559
and the precision at rank 10.

00:45:46.559 --> 00:45:49.960
And here are the results.

00:45:49.960 --> 00:46:00.900
So if we would have used only the clinical
EEG reports with no processing of the medical

00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:08.589
concepts, then we would have obtained a precision
of, then, at about 80%.

00:46:08.589 --> 00:46:13.619
And as you see the map, which we have defined
the mean average precision would be only about

00:46:13.619 --> 00:46:17.029
52%.

00:46:17.029 --> 00:46:25.710
If we would have used some language models
in which we would have tried to create a similarity

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:32.130
based on statistical properties between the
query and the EEG reports, actually, the mean

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:35.819
average precision would have been even worse.

00:46:35.819 --> 00:46:43.970
And as a third baseline, if we would have
used just simple text processing, the median

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:47.420
average precision would have been 46%.

00:46:47.420 --> 00:46:53.750
Therefore, in less than half of the cases,
the patient would not have been relevant.

00:46:53.750 --> 00:47:02.739
Our multimodal patient retrieval system, which
we call Mercury, obtained much better results

00:47:02.739 --> 00:47:07.630
because we were able first to introduce the
medical concept dictionary.

00:47:07.630 --> 00:47:14.140
So we obtain average precision of 58% to 57%.

00:47:14.140 --> 00:47:20.509
When we also introduced the signals, this
average precision jumped to 70%.

00:47:20.509 --> 00:47:25.450
But I also want to note this other metric,
which is the precision at 10, which means

00:47:25.450 --> 00:47:28.249
the 10 most relevant patients.

00:47:28.249 --> 00:47:33.420
And please note that in the case when we used
the signals as well as the processing of the

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:38.759
EEG reports, [INAUDIBLE] in a precision of
100%, meaning that the first 10 patients are

00:47:38.759 --> 00:47:45.200
always evaluated as should have belonged to
the cohort.

00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:53.069
When we have used only the automatically produced
annotations on the EEG report, the precision

00:47:53.069 --> 00:48:00.069
at 10 was 90%, meaning that out of 10 patients,
nine should have been in the cohort, and one

00:48:00.069 --> 00:48:05.539
is incorrectly included in a relevant cohort.

00:48:05.539 --> 00:48:15.170
So as summary, I hope that I have convinced
you that using deep learning on big data can

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:20.970
be transformative because active learning
can take advantage of [INAUDIBLE] successful

00:48:20.970 --> 00:48:28.039
deep learning architectures and approaches
to produce annotations, and in the case when

00:48:28.039 --> 00:48:33.049
adequate sampling mechanism are considered
in the active learning loop.

00:48:33.049 --> 00:48:39.309
Also, neural learning enables a large number
of annotation tasks be performed jointly and

00:48:39.309 --> 00:48:44.900
provides a very rich semantic information
to be captured in a global manner across vast

00:48:44.900 --> 00:48:45.900
clinical resources.

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:53.400
We believe that deep learning can play an
important role because data [INAUDIBLE] critical

00:48:53.400 --> 00:48:59.940
because there's lack of annotated data, and
there's many applications which will not support

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:09.670
the development of annotated big data resources--
they are developed for a small number of reports

00:49:09.670 --> 00:49:18.210
and a small number of types of concepts that
we aim to annotate.

00:49:18.210 --> 00:49:21.829
Annotations is just an enabler for many applications.

00:49:21.829 --> 00:49:28.210
For instance, searching through newly annotated
data, we also have to consider all these modalities.

00:49:28.210 --> 00:49:34.710
Sometimes, we have text narratives, but we
also might have other kind of signals or images

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:37.690
associated with them.

00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:44.920
The clinical information needs to be organized
into a multimodal index, which can be generated

00:49:44.920 --> 00:49:48.690
with novel deep learning representations.

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:54.940
The annotations produced with deep learning
in vast clinical data will enable the generation

00:49:54.940 --> 00:50:01.210
of probabilistic representations of medical
knowledge, which will give a new impetus to

00:50:01.210 --> 00:50:05.650
probabilistic medical inference.

