00:52:20.960 --> 00:52:27.339
underspecified information in EEG reports.

00:52:27.339 --> 00:52:31.730
So I would be glad to take questions now.

00:52:31.730 --> 00:52:33.980
So hi, [AUDIO OUT] Harabagiu.

00:52:33.980 --> 00:52:37.579
Thank you so much for being with us here today.

00:52:37.579 --> 00:52:42.840
You're taking time away from your day job
as professor, and we really appreciate it.

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:45.320
This work is fascinating.

00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:50.109
It seems like it's very much related to your
big data knowledge grants.

00:52:50.109 --> 00:52:56.579
I'm wondering what other applications do you
see of this because certainly, the methods

00:52:56.579 --> 00:53:02.640
that you've talked about and the usage of
deep learning and all these different-- in

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:07.793
multiple ways for the same problem, towards
the same goal, but using it multiple times,

00:53:07.793 --> 00:53:09.480
seems to be a very novel way of doing things,
same problem, towards the same goal, but using

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:18.180
it multiple times, seems to be a very novel
way of doing things, but yet also very-- could

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:24.970
be applicable in other [AUDIO OUT] of applications
or types of problems.

00:53:24.970 --> 00:53:29.089
What do you see doing next after this?

00:53:29.089 --> 00:53:31.970
What do you see as the next step?

00:53:31.970 --> 00:53:37.930
Well, what I would be interested is in using
these methods and probably developing new

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:45.410
methods to be able to do clinical decision
support and being able to identify how all

00:53:45.410 --> 00:53:56.259
these annotations can be used to inform questionanswering
methods that are trying to find similar cases

00:53:56.259 --> 00:54:03.599
in literature, especially as we have so many
publications being generated on a monthly

00:54:03.599 --> 00:54:09.060
basis and could inform a clinical decision
support.

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:15.680
Another important application would be this
creation of a very vast probabilistic knowledge

00:54:15.680 --> 00:54:21.970
base and sharing it with the community, especially
the machine learning community that is in

00:54:21.970 --> 00:54:31.960
big need of having the resources to apply
for probabilistic inference and creating factor

00:54:31.960 --> 00:54:38.799
graphs that would be able to give us priors
of all these clinical correlations and of

00:54:38.799 --> 00:54:41.539
similarities between patients.

00:54:41.539 --> 00:54:46.410
Another application that we have discussed
with our friends at Temple University is the

00:54:46.410 --> 00:54:52.029
ability to detect in real-time seizures.

00:54:52.029 --> 00:54:57.519
And this kind of very complex probabilistic
knowledge base would be able to inform very

00:54:57.519 --> 00:55:05.479
much these predictive methods that we could
develop on data that we annotate in any kind

00:55:05.479 --> 00:55:06.729
of corpus.

00:55:06.729 --> 00:55:11.250
This is not necessarily related to EEGs.

00:55:11.250 --> 00:55:16.710
Any kind of corpus that would have any kind
of clinical narratives and would have eventually

00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:23.460
any kind of other modality, like images, could
be processed with [INAUDIBLE] same methods

00:55:23.460 --> 00:55:30.599
with a little extra work of tweaking the annotation
schema to the specific problems that that

00:55:30.599 --> 00:55:34.900
corpus is covering.

00:55:34.900 --> 00:55:39.910
I'm glad you mentioned that one of the next
steps is doing the inference on here because

00:55:39.910 --> 00:55:46.089
one of the questions online is about how you
account for uncertainty, but in particular,

00:55:46.089 --> 00:55:54.140
how do you account for the uncertainty in
the data as it's collected, or that is collected.

00:55:54.140 --> 00:55:55.140
Exactly.

00:55:55.140 --> 00:55:57.359
So we know that this data is not perfect.

00:55:57.359 --> 00:56:01.160
So it may contain errors, and it has a lot
of uncertainty, definitely.

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:04.500
So there are two ways that we have in mind.

00:56:04.500 --> 00:56:11.410
So the first way that we study [INAUDIBLE]
is try to infer the underspecified information.

00:56:11.410 --> 00:56:17.220
So there's a lot of clinical data that is
not giving you the full picture.

00:56:17.220 --> 00:56:22.519
So there's a lot of underspecified things
that are not discussed that you want to discover,

00:56:22.519 --> 00:56:23.660
as well.

00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:28.549
The other aspect of it is that once that you
have this probabilistic knowledge base, you'll

00:56:28.549 --> 00:56:37.089
be able to start using a probabilistic inference
on it to be able to deal with uncertainly.

00:56:37.089 --> 00:56:43.019
In the knowledge base that we are constructing,
we are associating these modality values.

00:56:43.019 --> 00:56:45.979
We have more than these three that I have
discussed here.

00:56:45.979 --> 00:56:52.200
We have several attributes which are values
of uncertainty, like is this hypothetical,

00:56:52.200 --> 00:56:56.859
is this possible, is it associated with another
patient, and so forth.

00:56:56.859 --> 00:57:03.160
And all of those kind of certainty values
coming from the inferences that I detected

00:57:03.160 --> 00:57:11.599
in the narratives, I used in probabilistic
inference to generate the most likely outcome

00:57:11.599 --> 00:57:19.239
that you are looking for from this very vast
knowledge base.

00:57:19.239 --> 00:57:20.239
Thank you.

00:57:20.239 --> 00:57:27.180
There's another question online, or a couple
of other questions, really about which kinds

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:31.869
of tools are you using in your research, and
tools or data.

00:57:31.869 --> 00:57:36.950
So for example, are you using IBM Watson or
other tools?

00:57:36.950 --> 00:57:40.729
Are you using PubMed Central's corpus as your
