00:57:47.069 --> 00:57:48.069
OK.

00:57:48.069 --> 00:57:51.130
So that's an excellent question.

00:57:51.130 --> 00:57:59.579
So first of all, we are using clearly for
question-answering, we are not using Watson

00:57:59.579 --> 00:58:03.910
because I have a past work in question-answering
for about 20 years.

00:58:03.910 --> 00:58:08.349
I'm one of the authors of a book on question-answering.

00:58:08.349 --> 00:58:15.420
So in my lab, there's a lot of expertise on
building question-answering systems.

00:58:15.420 --> 00:58:21.940
We believe we can do it better because we
have our own tools of relevance of natural

00:58:21.940 --> 00:58:28.989
language processing and the ability to identify
and pinpoint complex questions and their answers.

00:58:28.989 --> 00:58:32.390
So these are our own tools.

00:58:32.390 --> 00:58:37.150
And we actually want to share them with the
research community.

00:58:37.150 --> 00:58:45.309
These tools are now upgraded with these deep
learning methods-- some of them I have described

00:58:45.309 --> 00:58:46.309
here.

00:58:46.309 --> 00:58:52.150
The deep learning libraries that we have used
were TensorFlow from Google.

00:58:52.150 --> 00:59:00.469
We experimented with several others, but we
all settled on TensorFlow because we find

00:59:00.469 --> 00:59:05.650
that they are very reliable and very well-documented.

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:12.599
Also, in terms of other natural language processing
method that we are using, we are using the

00:59:12.599 --> 00:59:13.780
GENIA corpus.

00:59:13.780 --> 00:59:17.069
We make use of UMLS.

00:59:17.069 --> 00:59:27.660
We make use of the PubMed Central archive
in order to tune the features that turn out

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:31.440
to be most informative to our methods.

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:39.559
And by the way, we would like to release a
